{
  "slug": "meet-lars-fredholm-principal-data-engineer-at-daana",
  "title": "Meet Lars Fredholm: Principal Data Engineer at Daana",
  "thesis": "An interview-format team introduction to Lars Fredholm (investor-turned-builder at Daana, previously Head of Data at Advisa and at Validio). His argument: data modeling has been forgotten because modern cheap storage/compute removed the short-term punishment for doing it badly, and tools/patterns like dbt and Medallion actively loosen modeling discipline — producing an unmaintainable mess about a year in. Daana's answer is automated, metadata-driven data modeling that starts from real-world business logic, producing an unambiguous model that both ingest (upstream) and metrics/BI (downstream) become a function of. He argues unambiguity is now a hard requirement because AI needs unambiguous input to produce reliable output, and warns against copying software-engineering modularity (microservices) wholesale into the data stack.",
  "relevance": "low",
  "mandates": [
    {
      "claim": "Data modeling must be metadata-driven and must start from the actual real-world (business) logic — this is the problem the product solves and the reason it is 'special'.",
      "layer": "DAB",
      "implication": "The business-logic model is the origin point of the build, not a derivative of what the source systems happen to emit. Build the DAB focal model from business concepts first; do not let source-system shape drive it."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Both upstream layers (ingest) and downstream (metrics layer, BI) essentially become a function of what Daana has defined.",
      "layer": "cross-cutting",
      "implication": "The modeling layer is the single authoring surface; ingest (dlt) configuration and the downstream metrics/BI surface (the generated Unified Star Schema) should be derived/generated from the model definition rather than hand-authored independently. Hand-editing generated ingest or generated star-schema output breaks the stated architecture."
    },
    {
      "claim": "To get unambiguous output from AI you must have unambiguous input; 'a dozen partially overlapping hand-crafted dbt models is not unambiguous input.'",
      "layer": "DAB",
      "implication": "Overlapping, redundant, hand-written model files are a defect, not a style preference. The model must have exactly one place where each truth lives — the design must make partially-overlapping duplicate definitions impossible, not merely discouraged."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The purpose of data modeling (Kimball, Data Vault etc.) is to transform raw data into a truthful and sustainable shape that satisfies consumption; 'sustainable' is the hard word, and its absence only becomes visible after about a year.",
      "layer": "DAR",
      "implication": "Judge the consumption layer against a ~1-year horizon, not against whether it satisfies today's query. Short-term correctness is not evidence of a good model."
    },
    {
      "claim": "dbt and Medallion are named as patterns/tools that loosen modeling discipline — they effectively say 'it's not that important how you write and layer your models, just make more of them, and everyone can do it.'",
      "layer": "cross-cutting",
      "implication": "Do not reproduce a free-form, proliferating model-file layout or bronze/silver/gold layering as the organizing principle. The three-layer DAS/DAB/DAR split must be enforced by tooling, not by convention that each engineer applies differently."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The failure mode of undisciplined modeling is concrete: 'No one knows what or where the truth lies, half of your models are redundant or orphaned, your code base has as many conventions as there are data engineers in the team.'",
      "layer": "process",
      "implication": "Three explicit acceptance criteria for the platform: truth is locatable, no orphaned/redundant models can accumulate, and conventions are singular and machine-enforced rather than per-engineer."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Building a data stack with the same modularity (microservices etc.) as a software application is overrated and makes it very hard to solve single-source-of-truth, to process massive data sets, and forces DEs into completely new ways of working.",
      "layer": "cross-cutting",
      "implication": "Do not decompose the platform into independently-owned microservice-like model units. Keep the model centralized enough that single source of truth and large-scale processing remain tractable."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Data Engineering should continue approaching Software Engineering, but 'it's a delicate maneuver that takes time and will be very costly if rushed.' Adopting SWE practices (most things DevOps) is 'mainly great'; the naive assumption is that DE equals SWE.",
      "layer": "process",
      "implication": "Adopt DevOps-style practice incrementally. Rushing full software-engineering ceremony onto the data platform is called out as a costly mistake."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Data quality testing that is limited to 'not null' and 'is unique' is insufficient; distribution analysis uncovers a wide range of data quality insights, and having an ANSI SQL starting point is very beneficial for compatibility.",
      "layer": "tooling",
      "implication": "Plan for distribution-based/statistical checks (e.g. Kullback-Leibler divergence) in addition to null/uniqueness assertions, and express them in ANSI SQL so they run across warehouse engines."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The worst inherited architecture failed because the storage format was chosen without checking engine support: raw data moved to Parquet on S3-equivalent storage while the DWH had to stay on on-prem SQL Server for security, and that SQL Server version could not prune when reading Parquet from S3, so all data had to be read on ingest.",
      "layer": "DAS",
      "implication": "Validate that the chosen ingest storage format supports predicate/partition pruning on the actual query engine and version in use. A format choice that the engine cannot push down into produces a more complex architecture with worse performance than the one it replaced."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The team is 'all very invested in modern agentic workflows, which makes the product advance at incredible speed', and Lars's current client engagement is 'building the data stack and implementing agentic workflows'.",
      "layer": "process",
      "implication": "Agentic workflows are treated as part of the delivered engagement, not just internal tooling — an implementation is expected to be built and operated with agents against the model."
    }
  ],
  "artifacts": [
    {
      "name": "Daana CLI",
      "kind": "cli-tool",
      "detail": "Named verbatim as \"Daana CLI\". Lars \"writes Go for the CLI on the side\" and \"In the evenings I usually build or explore some new feature in Daana CLI\". Implementation language: Go. No commands, subcommands, or flags are named anywhere in the post."
    },
    {
      "name": "dbt",
      "kind": "tool-named-negatively",
      "detail": "Called out twice as an anti-pattern: \"patterns and tools (looking at you, dbt and Medallion) that loosen the once so important discipline that rules data modeling\" and \"A dozen partially overlapping hand-crafted dbt models is not unambiguous input\"."
    },
    {
      "name": "Medallion",
      "kind": "architecture-pattern-named-negatively",
      "detail": "Named alongside dbt as loosening modeling discipline. No bronze/silver/gold terminology is used; only the pattern name \"Medallion\"."
    },
    {
      "name": "Kimball",
      "kind": "modeling-methodology",
      "detail": "Cited approvingly as an example of data modeling whose purpose is \"to transform raw data into a truthful and sustainable shape that satisfies consumption\". No Kimball constructs (facts, dimensions, grain, SCD) are named."
    },
    {
      "name": "Data Vault",
      "kind": "modeling-methodology",
      "detail": "Cited in the same breath as Kimball: \"(Kimball, Data Vault etc.)\". No hub/link/satellite constructs are named."
    },
    {
      "name": "Kullback-Leibler divergence",
      "kind": "statistical-method",
      "detail": "Implemented \"in pure ANSI SQL\" / \"in ANSI SQL for anomaly detection\". Presented as the example of going beyond \"not null\" and \"is unique\". The implementation itself is not shown in the post."
    },
    {
      "name": "ANSI SQL",
      "kind": "sql-dialect-requirement",
      "detail": "\"having an ANSI SQL starting point is very beneficial for compatibility\" — the stated reason for choosing ANSI SQL over engine-specific SQL for data quality / distribution analysis."
    },
    {
      "name": "not null",
      "kind": "data-quality-test",
      "detail": "Quoted verbatim as the baseline test engineers are \"often limited to\" — insufficient on its own."
    },
    {
      "name": "is unique",
      "kind": "data-quality-test",
      "detail": "Quoted verbatim alongside \"not null\" as the insufficient baseline."
    },
    {
      "name": "Parquet on S3-equivalent storage",
      "kind": "storage-format-and-location",
      "detail": "The failed-architecture case: raw data moved to \"Parquet on S3-equivalent storage\" while the DWH stayed on on-prem SQL Server."
    },
    {
      "name": "SQL Server (on-prem)",
      "kind": "warehouse-engine",
      "detail": "\"the DWH still had to be on SQL Server on-prem for security reasons. And SQL Server (that version) didn't support pruning when reading Parquet from S3, so all data had to be read on ingest\"."
    },
    {
      "name": "pruning",
      "kind": "query-engine-capability",
      "detail": "Named as the specific missing capability that \"essentially eliminat[ed] all benefits of Parquet\" — the engine could not prune when reading Parquet from S3."
    },
    {
      "name": "metrics layer",
      "kind": "architecture-layer",
      "detail": "Named as a downstream consumer: \"downstream (metrics layer, BI) essentially become a function of what Daana has defined\"."
    },
    {
      "name": "ingest",
      "kind": "architecture-layer",
      "detail": "Named as the upstream layer: \"Both upstream layers (ingest) and downstream (metrics layer, BI) essentially become a function of what Daana has defined.\" No ingestion tool (dlt or otherwise) is named in the post."
    },
    {
      "name": "Go",
      "kind": "implementation-language",
      "detail": "\"now writes Go for the CLI on the side\" — the Daana CLI is written in Go."
    },
    {
      "name": "daana.dev",
      "kind": "url",
      "detail": "\"Want to see what we're building? Check out daana.dev.\" Docs are maintained by the team (\"polish Daana's docs\")."
    },
    {
      "name": "Validio",
      "kind": "prior-company",
      "detail": "Data observability company where Lars \"built the kind of data quality monitoring most teams don't even know is possible\" — the origin of the distribution-analysis approach."
    },
    {
      "name": "Advisa",
      "kind": "prior-company",
      "detail": "Where Lars was Head of Data and was first hired by Siamak."
    }
  ],
  "quotes": [
    "Metadata-driven data modeling, starting from the actual real-world (aka business) logic, has so many advantages.",
    "AI will automate everything, but as with any automation, to get unambiguous output you want to have unambiguous input.",
    "A dozen partially overlapping hand-crafted dbt models is not unambiguous input and any AI will struggle to make reliable output of such data.",
    "Both upstream layers (ingest) and downstream (metrics layer, BI) essentially become a function of what Daana has defined.",
    "The main purpose for data modeling (Kimball, Data Vault etc.) is to transform raw data into a truthful and sustainable shape that satisfies consumption.",
    "Add to that patterns and tools (looking at you, dbt and Medallion) that loosen the once so important discipline that rules data modeling.",
    "A common example is trying to build a data stack with the same modularity (microservices etc.) as a software application. Doing so makes it very hard to solve problems around single source of truth, how to process massive data sets, and not to mention teaching DEs completely new ways of working.",
    "Distribution analysis can uncover a wide range of interesting data quality insights in data, and having an ANSI SQL starting point is very beneficial for compatibility."
  ],
  "openQuestions": [
    "The post never names the DAS / DAB / DAR layers, dlt, daana-cli as a command, or the Unified Star Schema. Nothing here specifies how the three layers are separated, named, or generated — that must come from other sources.",
    "Which distribution/statistical tests to implement beyond null and uniqueness is explicitly left to judgment: 'Depending on use case, more sophisticated tests will pay off.' Only Kullback-Leibler divergence is given as an example, and no implementation is shown.",
    "How far to take software-engineering practice adoption is left unresolved: DE 'should continue approaching Software Engineering, but it's a delicate maneuver that takes time and will be very costly if rushed' — the post gives no line for where modularity becomes harmful beyond the microservices example.",
    "What makes a model 'sustainable' is named as the critical property but never operationalized; the only stated test is the passage of about a year.",
    "Whether the metrics layer and BI are literally generated from the model or merely constrained by it is ambiguous — 'essentially become a function of' is not further specified.",
    "The Parquet/SQL Server anecdote implies engine-capability validation must precede storage-format choice, but the post gives no checklist or procedure for doing that validation."
  ]
